[[00:21:56]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1316s) Orchard Hills property. While there's understandably a lot of interest in future zoning, tonight's decision is strictly about annexation. It is about whether it makes sense for this parcel to be officially to officially join the city of Cheyenne. Under Wyoming State Statute 15-1-42, this property satisfies and exceeds all five of the state's legal criteria.

[[00:22:17]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1337s) First, contiguity. The property is immediately adjacent to the existing city limits, making this a logical, orderly expansion of the city's boundary rather than a leaprog development. Second, natural and integrated development. This land is already surrounded by high impact infrastructure. There are already three existing or planned electrical substations on the property along with a multitude of high voltage transmission lines. Furthermore, we are immediately adjacent to the 900 acre metadata center campus. From an infrastructure perspective, this land is already deeply integrated. Third, service extension.

[[00:22:50]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1370s) This property is uniquely plug-and-play. All required municipal utilities are already sitting at the property line. The infrastructure is ready and we will fund 100% of the costs to tie into those existing lines, ensuring no financial burden to Cheyenne taxpayers. Fourth,

[[00:23:05]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1385s) health, safety, and welfare. This is where the community wins. Unlike unlike

[[00:23:09]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1389s) other developments, data centers generate virtually no traffic. Furthermore, by annexing this property, we will help solve a major local transportation issue by facilitating the

[[00:23:19]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1399s) expansion of the Wallik and Parsley roads. This will directly relieve congestion at the new 56 grade school and improve traffic safety for thousands of surrounding residents. And to address neighbors concerns that we've listened to over the past couple of months, we are proposing a substantial 500 plus

[[00:23:36]](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-zBOAUpDE3E&t=1416s) foot buffer to shield adjacent properties that make better view sheds. And we are fully prepared to put our commitments to strict noise and zero spill lighting mitigations in writing as a binding condition of this project. Finally, utility integration. We are prepared to work with the city to establish necessary franchise utilities.

